# udev rule for /dev/alloyfs, the AlloyFS kernel module's control device.
# Installed by packaging/install.sh as /etc/udev/rules.d/60-alloyfs.rules.
#
# ## Why this file has to exist
#
# The module registers its misc device with mode 0600 owned by root
# (kernel/alloyfs/alloyfs_conn.c). That is the correct DEFAULT — it is the
# permission the node is created with by devtmpfs, before udev has had a
# chance to run, so there is never an instant in which the device is more open
# than intended. But it also means the only process that can open the device
# is root, while the AlloyFS agent deliberately runs unprivileged (the systemd
# template unit runs it as `User=%i`). Without a rule like this one, the
# kernel mount backend is simply unusable by the daemon it exists to serve.
#
# ## Why not just make it world-accessible
#
# Opening /dev/alloyfs makes the opener a filesystem SERVER. It answers every
# lookup, read, write and readdir for the alloyfs mounts bound to that
# connection, and it can inject fsnotify events into them. Anyone who can open
# the device and get a mount pointed at them therefore controls what other
# processes on this machine READ from a filesystem — which is a straight path
# to feeding attacker-chosen bytes to something that trusts its own disk, and
# to serving content that changes between one read and the next (a TOCTOU
# primitive against anything that validates a file and then re-opens it).
# It is also an unprivileged handle on kernel code that parses a binary
# protocol, so it is worth treating as attack surface in its own right.
#
# So: 0666 is out of the question, and so is a rule that hands the device to
# every logged-in user via uaccess/seat tagging. The device is a service
# endpoint, not a piece of desktop hardware; it does not belong to whoever
# happens to be sitting at the console.
#
# ## The rule
#
# Access is granted to one dedicated system group, `alloyfs`, which
# install.sh creates and leaves EMPTY unless you name a user
# (`install.sh --user alice`). Membership is the whole permission model:
# adding a user to this group is the deliberate act of saying "this account
# may serve alloyfs filesystems on this machine". Note that mounting is still
# separately privileged — an unprivileged server with no way to mount can only
# serve filesystems that root chose to point at it.
#
# Rule ordering: 60- puts this after systemd's own device rules (which set the
# baseline ownership) and before any local 70-/90- overrides, matching the
# convention for rules that adjust permissions on a specific device.

KERNEL=="alloyfs", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", GROUP="alloyfs", MODE="0660"
